According to a new report from Intel Market Research, the global prosthetic foot market was valued at USD 931 million in 2024 and is projected to reach USD 1,382 million by 2032, growing at a CAGR of 6.0% during the forecast period (2025–2032). This growth is driven by the rising global incidence of diabetic amputations and vascular diseases requiring lower limb prosthetics, coupled with technological innovations in prosthetic design.
A prosthetic foot is a specialized medical device designed to replace a missing lower limb, typically lost due to trauma, disease, or congenital conditions. These devices restore ambulatory function while mimicking the biomechanics of the anatomical foot. Modern prosthetic feet have transitioned significantly from traditional wood and steel to sophisticated materials like carbon-fiber composites and other advanced polymers.
